1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 20: satellite antennas

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:29,984
YA-NEC course, lesson 20: satellite antennas - Eggbeater, QFH and Quagi. Calculators, circular polarization.

3
00:00:30,034 --> 00:00:44,292
Eggbeater (F5VIF design, the 'tamago' variant) for 145.9 MHz: two crossed loops of 2157 mm, fed in quadrature by a 93 ohm lambda/4 line, radial reflector.

4
00:00:44,342 --> 00:00:59,451
41.6 + j0.3 ohm, 1.99 dBic at the zenith, axial ratio 4 dB, right-hand circular. Real time: SWR 1.2 and a broad, almost hemispherical pattern for LEO satellites.

5
00:00:59,501 --> 00:01:13,981
QFH, the quadrifilar helix, for the 137.5 MHz weather satellites.

6
00:01:14,031 --> 00:01:28,893
Large and small half-turn loops (Coppens' tables): 731 and 695 mm high. Real time: 4.81 dBi straight up, SWR 1.44 near 135.6 MHz.

7
00:01:28,943 --> 00:01:45,124
'Create the NEC model and check it'.

8
00:01:45,174 --> 00:01:54,724
The helix in 3D. The warning: the model feeds the two loops directly at the terminals; in reality you need the coiled-coax balun.

9
00:01:54,774 --> 00:02:13,460
For tracking: the Quagi of N6NB, quad driven element and reflector plus rod directors.

10
00:02:13,510 --> 00:02:28,248
8 elements at 144.3 MHz on a 4.2 m boom: SWR 1.03, 13.34 dBi, F/B 14.2 dB, beamwidth 39 x 43.5 degrees.

11
00:02:28,298 --> 00:02:40,513
A Quagi stays 0.3-0.6 dB below a DL6WU Yagi of the same boom (Cebik), but it is easy to build in wood. Two crossed Quagis give circular polarization.

12
00:02:40,563 --> 00:02:51,017
Eggbeater, QFH and Quagi: from fixed omni to tracked beam. Last lesson: dishes, prime focus, offset and horns. 73 de IC8TEM
